Third-party material
--------------------

This repository carries none, with one exception, and it is the file
next door. LICENSE is the Free Software Foundation's text of the AGPL,
reproduced verbatim: it carries its own permission -- copy and
distribute it verbatim, changing it is not allowed -- so it is the
licence rather than material licensed by it, and it is not offered under
the grant above.

Everything else in a checkout is written for this repository and covered
by that grant. Nothing is forked, nothing is vendored, and nothing is
copied in from elsewhere: no upstream code under other terms, no seeded
database dump, no Ansible collection -- the one the role calls,
community.docker, is named in requirements.yml and installed from
Ansible Galaxy. The check scripts read jinja2 and PyYAML; they import
them, they do not carry them, and those libraries reach you from their
own distributor under their own terms rather than from here.

What this repository DEPLOYS is a different question, and it is the one
that grows.

The stack's template, templates/Artificial_Intelligence.docker-compose.yml.j2,
names the images a run deploys: the LLM gateway with its database and
cache, the vector database, the chat interface and the agents around
them.

The compose file is where container images are named, and the
orchestrator pulls each named image from that image's own registry at
deployment time. They are not carried here, not redistributed here, and
not covered by anything here. They are not covered works of this
repository and this repository never distributes them, so nothing here
places them under the AGPL: it governs this work and works based on it,
and an image fetched from its own registry at deployment time is
neither. This repository grants no rights over any of them, and could
not: they are not its to grant. Every one of them keeps its own licence
entirely, whatever that licence is -- and complying with it is the
business of whoever deploys the stack, not something this repository
does or can do on their behalf.

No image is named in this file, and the template stays the place where
they are named, the authoritative record of what the stack pulls: a
second list here would drift away from it at the first change. The
exception would be an image whose own terms require a notice to travel
alongside the software, which is what a file like this one is for.

The role that deploys the stack is not here either.
"Artificial_Intelligence_Docker_stack_deployment_role_name" names it, the caller supplies
it, and it is resolved from the caller's own roles_path. It belongs to
whoever wrote it, under whatever terms they chose. That the stack can be
deployed by a role this repository does not contain is the design rather
than an omission, and it is why nothing in this file can speak for that
role's licence.


Attribution
-----------

Attribution is a licence obligation here, not a courtesy: AGPL-3.0
sections 4 and 5 require the copyright notice and the licence notices to
be kept intact in copies, and to be carried by modified versions.
Keeping this file, the LICENSE file and the SPDX headers in the source
files intact is what satisfies them.

"reuse lint" checks it on every pull request (#52), against a REUSE.toml
that declares the two families which cannot carry a header in line, each
with its reason:

  * templates/**, rendered onto the managed node -- a header
    there would end up copied into the deployed compose file, which is
    not a source file of this repository;

  * **/*.json, because JSON has no comment syntax at all. REUSE would
    also accept a "<file>.license" sidecar for those; declaring the
    extension once here is the choice made, rather than a second file
    next to every JSON the repository will hold.

LICENSE is not an exception any more: it holds the licence text, and
LICENSES/AGPL-3.0-only.txt, the file REUSE requires, is a symbolic link
to it, so the two are the same bytes. The link goes that way round
because GitHub did not recognise the licence while LICENSE was the
link: it reads a regular file.

One thing the check does NOT cover, and it is worth knowing here rather
than assuming otherwise: a root file whose name begins with LICENSE is
read as a licence text and is never asked for a header. Measured -- a
LICENSE-COMMERCIAL.md passes identically with one and without one. That
file's own header is therefore a matter of review, not of CI.
